# DECK 01 — a directory chapter whose subchapters are the SLIDES. Each slide carries `notes:`,
# the spoken script: the chapter renderer draws the title, `description` and the keyless body,
# and IGNORES every other keyed entry (tools/server/src/client/renderers/chapter-model.ts), so
# the script sits in the file and never on the screen. Sources for every claim below:
# docs/language/vs-yaml, ../../README.md §The core idea. Format rules: ../.yo/body.yo.
!!<*yamlover: $defs: chapter>
YAML vs yamlover
description: You already know the syntax — here is where the meaning parts ways
- You already know most of it
  notes: |
    Open examples/05-tour.yaml and leave it on screen for the whole slide.

    Say: this is ordinary YAML, and almost all of it is also yamlover. The surface is
    the same one you type every day — block and flow collections, both quote styles,
    block scalars, comments, the merge key, the float specials. Nothing was taken away
    and nothing needs converting.

    Then say: so the interesting part of this talk is small. It is four places where the
    same characters mean something different — and one place where the file stops being
    the boundary of the document.
  - |
    yamlover **keeps YAML's surface**. Block and flow collections, `'single'` and
    `"double"` quotes, `|` and `>` block scalars, comments, `<<:` merge keys, `.inf`
    and `.nan` — unchanged.
- Not a superset — a close twin
  description: A .yaml file is read as YAML; a .yo file as yamlover. No porting step.
  notes: |
    Show examples/05-tour.yaml and examples/06-tour.yo side by side — the same
    tour, once in each language.

    Say: yamlover is deliberately NOT a superset of YAML. It is a distinct, closely
    related language that shares the surface and differs in links and anchors. That
    sounds like a problem for your existing files, and it would be — except that
    reading is concrete-aware. The extension decides the semantics: a dot-yaml file is
    parsed with YAML's link rules, a dot-yamlover file with yamlover's.

    So your YAML is read faithfully, as YAML. There is no migration, and no dialect
    guessing.
  - |
    Two languages, one surface — and the **file extension** picks which semantics apply.
    Your existing YAML is read *as YAML*, faithfully.
  - !!<format: text/markdown> |     # a per-chunk format override — the code block needs one
    ```
    YAML  ≈  yamlover        # close, but different in links and anchors
    ```
- The same token, two meanings
  notes: |
    Stay on this slide — the table IS the content. Read the rows out loud, one at a time.

    Say: an ampersand in YAML names a node so an alias can reuse it inside that one
    document. In yamlover it is a PATH anchor — a claim that this node also lives at
    that path, and the path's parent really gains that key.

    A star in YAML is an alias to such a name. In yamlover it is a pointer, and it takes
    a whole path expression — a sibling key, a parent's position, a document root. No
    anchor namespace to look a name up in.

    Then the two smaller ones: double-bang-set in YAML is a mapping of null-valued keys;
    here it is a container with set semantics, where membership dedups by identity. And a
    scalar that also has fields is invalid YAML — two node kinds at once. In yamlover it
    is one ordinary node, and that is the next slide.

    Close by tapping the table itself: this grid is not pipe characters in a paragraph.
    It is a yamlover node tagged with the table schema, and only the cells are prose.
  - !!<*yamlover: $defs: table>
    header:
      - Token
        width: 2
      - In YAML
        width: 5
      - In yamlover
        width: 6
    - - '`&name`'
      - a reusable name for this node, inside this document
      - a **path anchor** — the node *also lives at* that path
    - - '`*name`'
      - an alias to the anchor called `name`
      - a **pointer** — a path expression, with no anchor namespace at all
    - - '`!!set`'
      - a mapping whose keys all have null values
      - a container with **set semantics** — membership dedups by identity
    - - a scalar with fields
      - invalid — a node is one kind or the other
      - '**one node** — a value and fields together, by default'   # a leading `*` needs quoting
- A pointer is a path
  notes: |
    Open examples/58-genealogy-dag and click through two or three people.

    Say: because a star carries a path instead of a name, a pointer can leave the file.
    It can name a sibling key, a position in the parent, a node in another document, a
    file on disk. The result is that a yamlover tree is really a graph — the same person
    node is reachable from both parents, and nobody had to copy it.

    Point at the upstream relations strip above the content: those are the incoming
    edges, computed from the pointers. A tree gives you one way in. This gives you all
    of them.
  - |
    A pointer names a **path**, not an anchor — so it can reach a sibling, a position,
    another document, or a file. That is what makes a yamlover tree a **graph** with
    shared nodes and cycles.
- One ordered container
  notes: |
    Open examples/07-omni.yo.

    Say: YAML has two container kinds — a sequence and a mapping — and a node must
    choose. yamlover has one. A mapping is ordered, positions are integer keys, and a
    keyless entry simply takes its position. Which means keyed and keyless entries can
    live in the same node, and a node can carry a scalar value alongside its fields.

    That mixture is not an exotic mode; it is the default, and it is exactly what a
    document needs. This deck's own slides are proof — every slide has a title, which is
    the node's own value, a notes field, which is keyed, and a body of chunks, which are
    keyless. Three facets, one node.
  - |
    No separate list and dict. A mapping is **ordered**, positions are integer keys, and
    keyed and keyless entries **mix in one node** — which plain YAML forbids.
  - !!<format: text/markdown> |
    ```yamlover
    a title of its own            # the node's self-value
    notes: what to say            # a keyed entry
    - the first body element      # a keyless entry — position 1
    ```
- A folder is a node
  notes: |
    Open examples/50-object-in-overlay, then 51-object-in-dir, then 52-scalar-as-file.
    Same data, three shapes on disk.

    Say: this is the part that has no YAML equivalent at all. An instance materializes
    two ways. As a file — one dot-yamlover file holding the whole thing. Or as a
    DIRECTORY, where the directory itself is the mapping: every file and subdirectory is
    an entry, the filename is the key, the bytes are the value.

    A directory can carry a hidden overlay, dot-yamlover slash body dot-yamlover, which
    adds values the filesystem cannot express and — as an array of pointers — imposes the
    order of the children. A bare directory just takes filesystem order.

    And the punchline: a file and a subdirectory are equivalent ways to write the same
    node. You can collapse a folder into one file, or explode a file into a folder,
    without changing what the data means.

    Say last: this deck is that. It is a folder, with one overlay file in it.
  - |
    An instance materializes as **one file** or as **a directory** — where the directory
    *is* the mapping, filenames are keys, and `.yo/body.yo` adds values and
    imposes child order.
  - |
    Collapsing a folder into a file, or exploding a file into a folder, does not change
    what the data means.
- Why a new dialect earns its keep
  notes: |
    Open any node and click across the yamlover and json5p tabs, then the schema tab.

    Say: the payoff is that your data stays as files and folders, in a format a person
    can read, with no proprietary database and no vendor to leave. Your editor works.
    Git works — diffs are readable, merges are text merges. Your backup tool works,
    because there is nothing to back up but files.

    And on top of those same files you get this: an app that renders them. Which is
    what the rest of this series is about. Next time — the six small jobs a day throws
    at you, and how each one turns out to be a plain file.
  - |
    Your data stays **files in folders**, human-readable, in git, with no proprietary
    store and no vendor to leave — and an app that renders them anyway.
  - Next up — mind-management in small doses
